{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game","id":761321,"title":"Changeling is not a \"light\" game.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-11-09T02:09:07-08:00","id":761321,"title":"Changeling is not a \"light\" game.","content":"The criticism of Changeling that I keep seeing is that it is too light to fit in the World of Darkness. It's true that anything that isn't Vampire deviates from the World of Darkness aesthetic that was created specifically for Vampire. Each game line is a separate entity with a separate approach--but each presents a different facet of a broken world. Vampire shows vulnerable people being preyed on with no one noticing, and the loss of humanity that comes from practicing violence even when it's necessary to survive. Werewolf's world is one that is literally dying. Mage's World of Darkness is an apparently bright and clean one, but that hides Orwellian puppet masters that define reality itself. Wraith in its roundabout way portrays death as the final oblivion we fear it to be.<br /> <br /> Changeling is so pessimistic that it portrays merely growing up as a kind of death. There is the solipsism of childhood and the narcissism of youth and then... nothing. Nothing that really matters. The World of Darkness is such a grim place that once the reality of an ordinary life foists itself upon a person, the spark within them is extinguished. Even in a relatively vampire-dense city you're probably not going to fall prey to one. But most people grow up. Most people live fairly long lives nowadays; the majority of one's life, then, one is transfixed like an insect on a display board, by banality. This may not be a fair representation of real life. But it seems like a suitable fate in the World of Darkness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 11-10-2015, 01:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-09T07:26:52-08:00","id":761476,"title":"","content":"I do agree, It is a game about the loss of innocence and holding onto that last little bit of childhood. I use fae in my game similar to the fae our of the Dresden books, even the nice ones are deadly and sadistic. I use them as NPC's in my vampire game to add a little spice and variety to just dealing with other vampires. Currently I have more Fae, Anansi, and Mummies than I have Werewolfs....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7535-chaifi","name":"Chaifi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7535_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-09T19:07:22-08:00","id":761934,"title":"","content":"The common complaint isn't really that CtD is too &quot;light&quot; of a game. It's that the CtD books do a horrible job of selling it as a dark game (compounded heavily by plenty of groups that play it lighter than the total package of the books paint). I think missing this distinction generally harms attempts to discuss CtD with its detractors.<br /> <br /> And it is important to note that even though there are plenty of dark parts of the core books, they suffer significantly from making the dark parts of the game either feel minor, or no fun to deal with. Banality isn't presented as a nuanced force that makes the darkness in CtD interesting. It's presented extremely inconsistently and as something to avoid rather than engage with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-09T20:10:00-08:00","id":761962,"title":"","content":"How long the game runs, and what age group the characters play as, matters a <i>lot</i>. A short (one or two session) game, where all the PCs are childlings (pre-teens) . . . that can be very lighthearted. Anything is possible, from talking stuffed animals, to having tea with a dragon, to dancing with pixies under the moon light.<br /> <br /> Alternately, that same game can go very, <i>very</i> dark. Borrowing an idea from the <i>Immortal Eyes</i> chronicle (and movies like <i>Sucker Punch</i>), imagine a bunch of childlings committed to a snakepit of an mental hospital by well-meaning parents. Nightmarish chimera spawned by the other patients, Autumn People trying to &quot;help&quot; them with electroshock therapy and powerful psychoactive drugs . . .<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, a long-term grump (adult changeling) campaign is going to be big on the depressing/pessimistic elements. They've already got a lot of Banality weighing them down, eating at their memories . . .and the world is always happy to give 'em even more. Depending on the Storyteller, in such a campaign, you're more likely to lose your PC to Banality and permanent amnesia, than to death. Which might hurt more, actually.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <i><u>Edit</u></i>: There are also the complications from trying to be both fae and human at the same time. Seanan McGuire explored some of them in <b>Rosemary and Rue</b>, the first of her &quot;October Daye&quot; novels (which read suspiciously like a variant <i>Changeling</i> campaign). One quest gone wrong, and the protagonist spends fifteen years trapped in the Dreaming. Fifteen years where her daughter and husband believed she ran out on them. . . and there's no way to explain to the bitter, angry, and (very heartbroken) duo what really happened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","user":"One Vorlon","date":". 11-09-2015, 11:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-09T23:03:58-08:00","id":762023,"title":"","content":"Good point, Heavy Arms. I personally got a creepy vibe from the saccharine presentation in the core book, but it could stand to be a bit more overt--assuming I wasn't imagining it entirely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-10T01:05:59-08:00","id":762075,"title":"","content":"Something I've said in the past, at least regarding the art of CtD, is that I don't think it needs to be more overtly dark. I think that misses the point. It needs to be more <i>subtly</i> dark; and consistently so.<br /> <br /> To give an example:<br /> <br /> The friendly balloon bear. As is, he's a problem with being too lighthearted. He's presence says, &quot;The Dreaming is an awesome fun fantasy land that is amazing and cool.&quot; It never communicates the potential darkness lurking in the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> Now imagine if the art had a piece where a balloon gets away, and the bear seems incredibly distraught. And another piece shows that inside each balloon is a dream of its own. Suddenly... each lost balloon is a dream lost into the Dreaming; maybe never to return. Now the Dreaming, no matter how saccharine it looks, is a realm of strange symbolism, and consequences completely out of proportion with their normal associated values.<br /> <br /> That little bit of art change, makes things a ton darker. It isn't hitting you over the head by turning balloon bear into pedobear, or some such nonsense. It's letting the art guide people to seeing that even in the bright shiny light of the happiest parts of the Dreaming, things are not good.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-10T01:37:19-08:00","id":762089,"title":"","content":"Most of my exposure to changeling was seeing larps full of people dressed as elves, cat people and ren fair folk. Generally they seemed jacked on pixi sticks and good vibes...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3953-dwight","name":"Dwight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3953_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-10T06:20:16-08:00","id":762245,"title":"","content":"This is why we need more Changeling. Now!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-10T07:32:42-08:00","id":762294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dwight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=762089#post762089\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most of my exposure to changeling was seeing larps full of people dressed as elves, cat people and ren fair folk. Generally they seemed jacked on pixi sticks and good vibes...</div> </div> </div> </div>The folks who ran the Changeling LARP at the GenCon I went to used Skittles for glamour.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-10T08:18:17-08:00","id":762335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>etherial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=762294#post762294\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The folks who ran the Changeling LARP at the GenCon I went to used Skittles for glamour.</div> </div> </div> </div>Great idea!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-10T16:57:14-08:00","id":762700,"title":"","content":"It's a difficult game to get right, simply because the practicalities of real human beings roleplaying tends to lend itself to either detective stories, or wild action adventure. Those two ways of playing tend to be the default, because they are by far the easiest for ordinary human being to ST. Anything else takes a lot of work on both the ST's part and the players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-11T09:39:47-08:00","id":763182,"title":"","content":"Here are my three points of contention on this issue, and know up front that *I* know that the following is completely unfair and may not actually reflect my own feelings. That said, also know that I only bring these up because they are mentioned above, which caused me to think of the reasons <b>Changeling: the Dreaming</b> is NOT a dark game, if not a proper 'light' game.<br /> <br /> 1. The game as written hinges on an impossibly idealised image of childhood.<br /> <br /> This is a core concept all over the core book (both editions), and that very notion of 'grown up equals bad' and 'childhood equals good' sets a contrast that isn't allowed much nuance in the core books. Oh sure, there are options found elsewhere (please see below), but that borderline infantilism vibe is off-putting for many of us more familiar with other iterations of the World of Darkness. Yeah, the 'loss of innocence' bit is tragic, but it's also part of growing up in the Real World. That makes it a little hard for some to take seriously as a 'horror' or 'dark' game. So, the WORST thing that can happen to a changeling is to fall to banality, which makes that character a *<i>gasp</i>* human being just like me! Oh, the dark and oh, the horror...<br /> <br /> 2. Bright colours cannot exist in the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> All of the art in the core book (and yeah, I keep hitting this unfairly, but I'll get to that point) is full colour, bears and clowns and sad 'monsters' in full daylight. Hell, even the Sluagh and Redcaps don't come across as terribly dark in that book, despite us being told their emo and savage, respectively. The system of gaining Glamour doesn't hurt anyone or anything, and the notion of their 'fallen world' is, well, OUR world rather than a <i>worse</i>-than-our-world presented in, say, <b>Werewolf</b> where the whole world is ending, <b>Vampire</b> where the whole world is ending, or <b>Mage</b> where, um... The whole world is ending? Maybe that gets old, but in <b>Changeling</b> it comes down to far more personal stakes, something that isn't seen as so much 'dark' by some of us.<br /> <br /> 3. The core book doesn't encompass the entire line in terms of theme, mood, or overall milieu.<br /> <br /> THIS is why I'm so unfair above (and ultimately why I'm right chuffed for <b>Changeling 20</b>). <b>Vampire</b> and <b>Werewolf</b> and all through the rest of the classic World of Darkness lines present a vision of the game that is expanded upon and 'focused' by supplements growing from the initial book's presentation. <b>Changeling: the Dreaming</b> did this quite differently, which was actually brilliant in its own way of 'drawing out the darkness' form. However, it also made it terribly difficult for a 'casual' observer (read: one who only looked at the core book) to see many nuances.<br /> <br /> ...So yeah, I fall closer to the OP's point of view on this than the opposite, but the above does show how I saw the game at one time. My HOPE is that the Twentieth Anniversary Edition/Revised version will address these by giving a more holistic view of the <b>Changeling</b> milieu.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>B.I.G. Bird spreads the word: Anybody with a heart votes love<br /> <a href=\"https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/</a></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/409-phaolan","name":"Phaolan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar409_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"478"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-12T07:40:24-08:00","id":764043,"title":"","content":"The difference in Changeling is that players play hope.<br /> <br /> Vampires scheme against each others in the darkness.<br /> Wolves have already perpetrated a genocide against others change-forms.<br /> Technomages have eradicated mysticism and are making magic disappear.<br /> <br /> Changeling have to fight for their world, but to do so, they had to leave it, maybe for ever.<br /> They were fae, now they are just half of it.<br /> They forgot who they were, the only fragments of memories they keep, come through greymare.<br /> If they spend too much time in fae form, they get mad.<br /> In Changeling, the shadow court and the nightmares are monsters that kill people and changeling alike.<br /> But people are the one that kill the Dream.<br /> By making reality shows, reusing the same songs over and over, making everything mainstream.<br /> <br /> However, they can fight, they can make people think about what is true or not.<br /> They have to make people dream.<br /> <br /> So yeah, Changeling is the spark in the shadows of the world of darkness, but there is nothing light about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8693-pilodermann","name":"Pilodermann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8693_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-12T10:42:02-08:00","id":764192,"title":"","content":"I think C20 will be a hard one to do right, because the core mechanics of CtD were...insufficient...at best, to dealing with the nature of the setting they seem to want to get across. (And that's leaving out the literally incomplete mechanics that exist in a few places.)<br /> <br /> The biggest thing - and I've said it before - that I would do, if I were writing the rules for a CtD system, is have glamour and banality both be <i>resources</i>. Banality would offer genuine &quot;dark gifts&quot; for embracing your humanity, your mortality, and the harsh realities of the world. Being lost to Banality is horrible, yes, but I would re-emphasize the risks of being lost to the Dreaming, as well. Being unable to recall your mortality, being bound too strongly by the sorts of things reflected in the extant system's Echoes flaw.<br /> <br /> But at the same time, gaining glamour would be a little easier; obeying the strictures of the fae, doing things which ignore reality's conveniences for the fantastic limitations of the Dreaming, these would give you tokens of glamour. Dross would be more prevalent as a way to bolster your personal reserves. Because as-is, using Cantrips was...painfully difficult and costly, and so I found that you more often played a mortal who was in a shared game of &quot;let's pretend&quot; with other fae, rather than an actual magical being in a mundane world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-12T11:11:21-08:00","id":764220,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pilodermann</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=764043#post764043\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The difference in Changeling is that players play hope.</div> </div> </div> </div>Great point... And for too many of us who cut our teeth on <b>Vampire</b> or <b>Werewolf</b> (is that a pun?), 'hope' is synonymous with 'light and fluffy' in our minds. It's kind of odd that <b>Hunter: the Reckoning</b> didn't invoke a similar feel, but I'd hazard a guess that's 'coz the monsters were still the recognisable levels of monstrous in <b>Hunter</b>.<br /> <br /> ...Still, if *I* can change my mind about <b>Changeling: the Dreaming</b>, it's possible that others may, too! Ultimately, this is why I look forward to <b>C20</b>, to share that experience with as many as I possibly can.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>B.I.G. Bird spreads the word: Anybody with a heart votes love<br /> <a href=\"https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/</a></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/409-phaolan","name":"Phaolan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar409_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"478"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-12T13:18:37-08:00","id":764337,"title":"","content":"Changeling newbie here, so forgive me if I come across as ignorant of the game and its themes or mechanics, I'm still new to Dreaming.<br /> <br /> From what I have read, Changeling: The Dreaming is outwardly lighthearted but inwardly hopelessly dark. But is there a way to make Changeling not hopeless? Does Winter have to be endless? Can it not be finite? I want to get into Changeling and maybe even ST it, but I am afraid that Changeling may be too dark despite its bright and colorful exterior. If you were going to &quot;fix&quot; Changeling without making it hopelessly grimdark, how would you do it? From what I have read of the gameline, it had a unique flaw in that it was both too dark and too light at the same time and in lopsided ways.<br /> <br /> Forgive me if I am missing the point, I just want to figure out this game that I really want to get into, and see what I need to tweak and what I need to keep. After all, I'm the kind of Storyteller who focuses more on the action-adventure aspects of World of Darkness, and I am wondering if Changeling can pull this off. I have done it with Werewolf, Mage (well, I planned out a Mage game, never got to run it), and even Vampire, and I am hoping I can run some adventurous chronicles with Changeling: The Dreaming","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","user":"Camilla","date":". 11-12-2015, 04:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-12T13:55:31-08:00","id":764368,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pilodermann</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=764043#post764043\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The difference in Changeling is that players play hope.</div> </div> </div> </div>How do the books actually sell this though? Unseelie are not the evil faction (that's the Shadow Court). Unseelie are supposed to be playable from the get go. They are certainly not a group with &quot;hope&quot; as a core idea of what you're playing.<br /> <br /> This also seems to skip the sheer amount of damage the Changelings have done to themselves; such as the Accordance War. Changelings are presented as pretty prone to getting caught up in fantasy, politics, and internal warfare even as winter is on the horizon.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But people are the one that kill the Dream.<br /> By making reality shows, reusing the same songs over and over, making everything mainstream. </div> </div> </div>And this pretty much sums up a lot of why people have trouble taking the darkness in CtD.<br /> <br /> Humanity can't be killing the Dream if humanity is responsible for the Dream existing. This sort of nuance lacking concept of Banality ruins the potential darker aspects of the Changeling metaphysics. It never reconciles things like how on earth the moon landing, which is supposed to have been one of the greatest releases of Glamour in the modern age, emerges from Banality 10 Dream murdering laboratories that made the technology that allowed travel to the moon possible.<br /> <br /> It also doesn't jive with human history at all. Humans have been enjoying cheap mindless entertainment since well before what changelings consider the end of the mythic age. Why was popular entertainment in ancient Sumer not a problem of banality, but today it's a force of Dream-destruction? Instead of making the game dark, it just makes the game... well... hipster. It follows a fairly annoying logic of &quot;mainstream = bad, non-mainstream = good, even if it was something that used to be mainstream.&quot; that isn't dark. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Phaolan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=764220#post764220\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's kind of odd that <b>Hunter: the Reckoning</b> didn't invoke a similar feel, but I'd hazard a guess that's 'coz the monsters were still the recognisable levels of monstrous in <b>Hunter</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hunter, kind of like Werewolf, presents itself under the idea that &quot;winning&quot; is probably a lost cause, and thus &quot;losing as well as possible&quot; becomes the order of the day. This puts a big wet blanket on hope, because hope seems rather irrational in a world you have long odds of simply saving the world when the final battle of the End Times comes, let alone actually fixing it, or pretending you're going to see whatever happens after. Hunter is pretty strong on the idea that the Imbued are probably too little, and too late, to make a difference in the grand scheme of things.<br /> <br /> The further you get into Hunter, you also start running into an ongoing issue of the Imbued being pawns of larger forces, ready to be sacrificed in a cosmic chess game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-12T13:58:45-08:00","id":764372,"title":"","content":"Changeling: the Dreaming is about hope vs. despair, imagination vs. reality, and innocence vs. experience.<br /> <br /> On the surface, its stories are a combination of medieval-ish fantasy-adventure/fantasy-politics with a struggle to keep this extra life going in the face of real-world concerns. Or, it's a game about being normal people with an extraordinary secret and secret powers to go with it, and trying to keep the fun and excitement without letting it get in the way of your normal life.<br /> <br /> If you want to focus on action-adventure, Changeling's Dreaming-side is going to be a good home for you. Focus on the intrigue and adventure of dealing with chimerical threats and fantasy-adventure. The mundane world becomes a place you're protecting from these threats which most people can't even see. If you interact with werewolves, mages, or vampires, they are, to you, just strange chimerical threats which CAN be seen by the real world (but don't want to be). Bring your glamour and magics with you, and cultivate your dross so that you can remain potent; mages don't need &quot;juice&quot; to work, and vampires feed far more easily than you do. Werewolves...are terrifyingly potent, even without their Gifts.<br /> <br /> Like all WoD splats, you're living a double life as a Changeling. It's just more literal for you than most, because you're straddling two worlds, and it takes effort to bring one into the other. But both are real to you, and the politics of other Kinain inevitably impact your life in both worlds, because they, too, exist in both.<br /> <br /> <br /> As for whether Winter need be eternal...<br /> <br /> When I ran a Changeling game, I had the villain be a Sidhe youth that the party had some passing attachment to who, during a ceremony that he'd tried to avoid but which greatly offended his ancient Sidhe soul, erupted into his Unseelie mein. The Lord of Winter is a long-haired pretty-boy of a Sidhe who has worked for centuries, across multiple lifetimes, ever since the Shattering, to bring about Winter. Not because he's suicidal, but because he believes that this prolonged Autumn is killing the Fae, just as an eternal fall would lead to the death of plant and animal life alike. Winter, he believes, is not to be feared, but endured, or even embraced as a time of rest. For, after Winter, does not the Spring always come?<br /> <br /> So the question of its necessary permanence is a good one! Answer it how you will, as ST, to make your game more interesting and enjoyable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-12T17:30:37-08:00","id":764552,"title":"","content":"I agree with the core book being the opposite to many of the supplements, in many ways. The artwork doesn't help in that regard.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-13T01:16:26-08:00","id":764772,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=764368#post764368\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do the books actually sell this though? Unseelie are not the evil faction (that's the Shadow Court). Unseelie are supposed to be playable from the get go. They are certainly not a group with &quot;hope&quot; as a core idea of what you're playing.<br /> <br /> This also seems to skip the sheer amount of damage the Changelings have done to themselves; such as the Accordance War. Changelings are presented as pretty prone to getting caught up in fantasy, politics, and internal warfare even as winter is on the horizon.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seelies are seeking to restore the dream as it was. They are the hope that the Dream will survive.<br /> Unseelies are trying to live in the human world. So even if the dreaming is lost, it is not the end of faeries.<br /> <br /> i know unseelies are cool, I am playing one since 3 years, an unseelie sluag with a ghost who was his girlfriend (Plot twist, I had her raised from the dead by a salubri at the end of the second year) and know I have a dead 8-year old boy who was my son in one of my past lives.<br /> <br /> The humans are not only killing the dream by making art mainstream, they are also doing it by being less and less mystical and superstitious. However, the humans are also the reason why the dream exists because they can not explain everything.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8693-pilodermann","name":"Pilodermann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8693_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-13T03:18:38-08:00","id":764812,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pilodermann</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=764772#post764772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unseelies are trying to live in the human world. So even if the dreaming is lost, it is not the end of faeries.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not really hope though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The humans are not only killing the dream by making art mainstream, they are also doing it by being less and less mystical and superstitious. However, the humans are also the reason why the dream exists because they can not explain everything. </div> </div> </div>Again though, this sort of nonsensical approach of how humanity and the Dreaming interact completely undercuts the darkness of the game. Why is mainstream art bad? Mainstream art inspires thousands of people to dream of being stars, and trying art for themselves. Just like the moon landing, it offers a contradictory message that doing big impressive and inspiring stuff is awesome, but all the hard work of getting it there is horrible and evil. That's not dark, it's just kind of stupid.<br /> <br /> And the idea that humanity is somehow becoming less mystical and superstitious isn't really true either. It both romanticizes the state of such things in the past, and completely cherry picks who gets to count in the present.<br /> <br /> To avoid any major controversies, I'm going to stick to a bit of IRL experience with this. I spent some time working as an assistant for one of the members a hospital's alternative medicine committee. Basically, the committee decided if a given form of alternative medicine would fall into four categories. (1) It's scientifically valid, even if not the recommended treatment, so it will be practiced with the hospital's approval, (2) there's no scientific basis, but it isn't disruptive to accepted treatments/harmful to the patients, and the patients to can it on their own if they want, (3) it isn't harmful to the patient, but is disruptive to other patients and/or staff, and will not be allowed, and (4) it's harmful and hell no.<br /> <br /> When you start looking up what people are willing to do when medical science says, &quot;Sorry, we don't have anything we can do but make you comfortable,&quot; we can think we're living in a highly rational and reasoned society, but people will make up almost anything to convince themselves that if they do it, they can do what medicine can't. Which is pretty much invented on the spot superstition. <br /> <br /> What it really comes down to, is that humanity hasn't really changed in terms of why and when we experience the need for such things. It's that we try as hard as possible to avoid the causes. And the causes actually make for a much darker game. Comfortable humans, are humans that don't reach out to mystical and superstitious thinking. Those modes of thinking are part of our natural responses to living in highly stressful conditions.<br /> <br /> So, when you look at it from the idea that sucky lives (and the WOD is full of those) is what inspires humans to be less rational minded, and more superstitious, it makes for a much darker game where the protagonists want people to be more superstitious.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-13T04:25:27-08:00","id":764843,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=764368#post764368\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Humanity can't be killing the Dream if humanity is responsible for the Dream existing. This sort of nuance lacking concept of Banality ruins the potential darker aspects of the Changeling metaphysics. It never reconciles things like how on earth the moon landing, which is supposed to have been one of the greatest releases of Glamour in the modern age, emerges from Banality 10 Dream murdering laboratories that made the technology that allowed travel to the moon possible. . .</div> </div> </div> </div>Humanity is not <i>killing</i> the Dreaming. As a tangible manifestation of humanity's dreams and beliefs, that's probably not possible without killing every human on the planet (or maybe turning them all into mindless drones). Instead, humanity is <i>sterilizing</i> the Dreaming. Paving over the Smurf Village to put in <i>another </i>strip mall. Capturing all the unicorns, and offering 2-for-1 rides on their backs (infants ride free!). Dumping trash all over the sunken city of Atlantis, and (accidentally) wiping out the mermaids with dragnet fishing.<br /> <br /> The conflict between Glamour and Banality is basically the difference between wild possibility and boring predictability. Glamour is raw potential, wild magic made manifest. Anything is possible with it, from rainbow fairies dancing in the moonlight, to an albino tentacle horror exploding from the ground and eating out your eyes. Good, evil, or just insane, Glamour only cares whether something is amazing and wondrous. Its the stuff of dreams, nightmares, and raw inspiration.<br /> <br /> Banality, on the other hand, is boring and repetitive. Today will be like yesterday, and tomorrow will be like today. Banality has no room for rainbow fairies . . . or albino tentacle horrors. Sure, it may be dull, and toxic to the fae, but its also <i>safe</i>. And in the World of Darkness, safe is quite attractive. Stay in your box, and nothing from outside will come in and threaten you. <br /> <br /> That's why the moon landing was such a big deal. The Technocracy may promote Banality (although they might disagree with you on that), but going to the moon was a BIG DEAL for humanity. Suddenly, the idea of going to another world was <i>real. </i>Moving to another planet, meeting aliens . . . or having aliens come <i>here</i>. The moon landing showed the whole world that it was <i>possible</i>. It knocked a billion people out of their repetitive lines, and mindless repetition. Questions had to be asked, and radical new possibilities considered. It was a huge, huge win for Glamour, and a massive defeat for the pull of Banality.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-13T08:15:01-08:00","id":764952,"title":"","content":"Well, I have been reading CtD books since last year. The thing is that in start my mates had so much prejudge about the setting, and pick other settings like Werewolf or Vampire. Their most strong argument was Changeling's lack of darkness. However, I guess this is only because of the style of changeling books &amp; how art implemented.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7562-kokorowatari","name":"KokoroWatari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7562_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-13T10:58:26-08:00","id":765047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=764843#post764843\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Humanity is not <i>killing</i> the Dreaming.</div> </div> </div> </div>That was sort of my point. As well the rest of your post touched on what I was talking about with the relationship of comfort (or as you put it, predictability) to superstition. <br /> <br /> As for the rest of your post, I think CtD would be a much better game at communicating its themes if it actually presented things (in totality) that way; but it tries and fails to implement that successfully.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-13T21:10:27-08:00","id":765331,"title":"","content":"I would say that Changeling is a far darker more depressing game than Werewolf, Mage and Mummy. Leaving it in the middle of the pack as far as theme goes.Wraith, Vampire and Hunter being the top three as far as being dark.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3075-coridan","name":"Coridan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-13T23:46:41-08:00","id":765396,"title":"","content":"It fails to execute its dark side in a compelling manner because a lot of its dark side really does come from a nostalgic longing for one's childhood and a cynical fear of adulthood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-14T01:06:18-08:00","id":765421,"title":"","content":"Pan's Labyrinth is an incredibly dark story set in a very Changlingish world, so it's not like the setting can't be used for darkness. It just takes work.<br /> <br /> &quot; Even when I was a kid, funny enough, I used to be able to find those fairy tales that felt preachy and pro-establishment, and I hated them. I hated the ones that were about, \u2018Don\u2019t go out at night.\u2019 There are fairy tales that are created to instill fear in children, and there are fairy tales that are created to instill hope and magic in children. I like those. I like the anarchic ones. I like the crazy ones. And, I think that all of them have a huge quotient of darkness because the one thing that alchemy understands, and fairy tale lore understands, is that you need the vile matter for magic to flourish. You need lead to turn it into gold. You need the two things for the process. So when people sanitize fairy tales and homogenize them, they become completely uninteresting for me.\u201d-<b>Guillermo del Toro</b><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://movies.about.com/od/panslabyrinth/a/pansgt122206.htm\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://movies.about.com/od/panslabyrinth/a/pansgt122206.htm\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://movies.about.com/od/panslabyrinth/a/pansgt122206.htm\" target=\"_blank\"> http://movies.about.com/od/panslabyrinth/a/pansgt122206.htm </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3598,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","user":"CajunKhan","date":". 11-14-2015, 04:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-14T12:20:19-08:00","id":765704,"title":"","content":"It is a matter of emphasis and presentation. The core rulebook presents a kind of high fantasy setting hidden behind our own world (technically the World of Darkness, although it doesn't really do a good job of creating the Gothic-Punk milieu for that setting). <br /> <br /> Later supplements ranged from urban fantasy to dark fantasy, although, like Wraith, much of the setting in the books seems to take place away from the World of Darkness somewhat in its own fantasy realm (freeholds, chimerical reality, the Dreaming), even where the two overlap.<br /> <br /> I like the breadth of possibilities in the Changeling setting. But I think the core rulebook should work better at deciding which angle it's going for, and to present it clearly. Or rather, to make clear the distinct possibilities for the game in terms of mood, so that the different styles of play are evident without having to read multiple sourcebooks.<br /> <br /> I imagine if it had received the Revised Edition treatment, we'd have seen gritty urban fantasy presented in the core book, rather like Mage, with a greater emphasis on the external threats of the Denizens, Dauntain and Fomorians. As it was, Changeling 2e seems to focus primarily on changeling politics and society, with Banality as an indistinct behemoth in the background, hence the feeling that it's kind of detached from the World of Darkness and existing in a sort of high fantasy alternate world all on its own.<br /> <br /> One way to do it would be to break the book down in a way similar to Unknown Armies 2e. Start off with the street-level stuff, the gritty stories, the everyday changeling stuff. Then go onto the courtly stuff and the international politics. Then, finally, take on the cosmic (or cosmological) stuff. M20 is probably a good model too.<br /> <br /> I think that would allow players to see the different styles of game, and the different ways they can tailor it to fit the kinds of moods they want to play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-14T12:37:26-08:00","id":765719,"title":"","content":"I think there are issues besides emphasis and presentation at hand. There's a lot of mechanics at hand that get in the way of things that probably need to be addressed. It'll be hard to change the emphasis especially if things like the current Glamour and Banality rules don't get a significant revision.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-15T20:24:54-08:00","id":766789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=765719#post765719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think there are issues besides emphasis and presentation at hand. There's a lot of mechanics at hand that get in the way of things that probably need to be addressed. It'll be hard to change the emphasis especially if things like the current Glamour and Banality rules don't get a significant revision.</div> </div> </div> </div>Where Banality occasionally gets muddled is when (usually First Edition) supplements muddle Banality, the Fomorian Dream and the Dauntain. At times it's static, evil and magical all at once. Which is probably right, but then the simple presentations of Banality that often lead to the assumption that all science must be bad, become reductive and need to be reworked to show that it's not a simple dichotomy.<br /> <br /> But I have no problem with rote and repetition (such as that which helps us fly to the Moon) being Banal while the spectacle or specific eureka moments that culminate from such repetition generate Glamour. To use Mage terms, Stasis is required to create patterns which allow Dynamism to take shape and have any kind of longevity. Glamour and Banality should have a fraught relationship, with one sometimes leading to another, with Ali the tragedy inherent in that (let's steal Vampire's Riddle and adapt it: 'Banal I must be lest Banal I become.').<br /> <br /> Some Banality is needed so that Glamour can have shape and meaning. But this means, as you pointed out earlier, I think, that there should be some tangible benefit for Banality too, so that it's not always a cartoon evil.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 11-16-2015, 06:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-15T21:20:44-08:00","id":766825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Segev</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=764192#post764192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think C20 will be a hard one to do right, because the core mechanics of CtD were...insufficient...at best, to dealing with the nature of the setting they seem to want to get across. (And that's leaving out the literally incomplete mechanics that exist in a few places.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm still optimistic, since we've seen from the Mage20 book that there's a definite willingness to alter certain things as necessary (I recall reading a post somewhere about the VtM 4E announcement that flat out said M20 and the Revised convention books were experiments in stretching the old material into something new in preparation for VtM4e, and IIRC, it also hinted that the C20 book would follow similar ground). At the very least, I'm hopeful that the incomplete mechanics you mention will be completed when they sit down and look at it.<br /> <br /> To the main point, I never really played CtD before, but I've read some articles that bring up the darkness inherent in Changeling. I recall the first I read on the topic arguing, quite well, that Changeling could be the darkest of the games because the best case scenario is you live old enough to forget all the magic you had; even wraiths have more hopeful ends than that, and even the Garou have the chance to live happy-ish lives if they're lucky enough (and if they are the kind that revel in constant warfare, their best end may well be their death in a glorious fashion). YMMV, of course, but it was definitely an eyeopening piece, and I wish I had saved it (and the other post I mentioned above).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-15T22:12:01-08:00","id":766842,"title":"","content":"*plunks two cents on the table*<br /> <br /> Spoilers, I'm going to do comparisons to Lost(the nwod game, not the show).<br /> <br /> Why?<br /> <br /> Well, here's the thing. Changeling, in either incarnation, is about the death of innocence* and what to do about it. More specifically, it's about when the sense of endless possibility and the actual attainment of joy mixed with a sense of simplicty is betrayed, and how hard it can be to find that feeling again, even in new and quieter forms. Both of these games are brutal in how they do this, with Lost perhaps being subtler while still beaning you in the head, but Dreaming is upfront about this...and more importantly, it's also fuller than Lost.<br /> <br /> It's not a <i>dark</i> game at it's core, though it can be-the fae and the banal have enough horror and grimness to eat at you. But Dreaming is more sinister than that. Dreaming is a <i>bleak</i> game. Lost is an autumn game where Dreaming is a winter game. Because, at the end of the day, it's not dread and fear that drives the game, but despair and fatalism. By hyper-aggrandizing the feeling of loss of innocence into a high fantasy story and then juxtaposing it with the grey reality, Dreaming's setting allows for harsh twists, turns, and heights.<br /> <br /> The big thing to it is that you're invited to explore the entire process, getting to explore a crazy magical land and know secrets others don't, then get traumatized by the fae and suffer as the banal finds ways to sink it's teeth into you, and it slowly shreds away at you until you're at a tipping point, and you have to decide-what am I going to do to keep my life special? How do you escape being a cog in the machine and truly <i>be</i>?<br /> <br /> Dreaming is accepting that the world hurts in a subtle, sinister way-that our dreams often will never be, and learning to accept some of it, defy other parts of it, and ultimately find a new way to keep a small fire alive.<br /> <br /> In fact, simply put, Dreaming is about any small thing that keeps you alive-that keeps your life worthwhile.<br /> <br /> It's a bleak, bleak game. But a quietly hopeful one too. One that needs silly bears with balloons somewhere in a hopelessly grey city, I feel.<br /> <br /> *Well, one of the things it's about in either incarnation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 12-11-2015, 10:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-16T07:04:16-08:00","id":767038,"title":"","content":"I am also hopeful for C20. I look forward to it. I just acknowledge that it's going to need a fair bit of work and willingness to tinker with the system to make it live up to the potential the original didn't quite achieve. Note: I really LIKE C:tD; that's why I get disappointed by the flaws I find in it. ^^; I've run a game despite them, and would probably do so again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-16T18:15:46-08:00","id":767492,"title":"","content":"I love running and playing CTD. I've got a whole slew of house rules, but broken rules I can fix. It was the setting that won me over.<br /> <br /> Still, I'm very curious about C20.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-18T05:56:22-08:00","id":768441,"title":"","content":"Speaking as someone who is only tangentially interested in Changeling, but who enjoyed reading this conversation, allow me to voice a couple of points.<br /> <br /> 1. I see great potential in the &quot;laboring futile against being crushed by the grey monotony of reality&quot; angle, but its loss state is, to echo a previous poster, nothing but mere reality. And as we find wonder (and i hope all of us do) in real life, and we live relatively fulfilled and happy lives, I cant fully buy that as a loss state.<br /> <br /> 2. This is a terribly smug thing of me to say, but just from looking, I feel like I am too old for it. I'm now 29, and my life is more or less on track. Were I my 22 year old self, just at the cusp of true independent adulthood, Changeling would hit way closer to home.<br /> <br /> 3. I actually like autumn and winter, and I like the melancholy inherent in these metaphors. To speak in Wraith terms, I never got why &quot;Oblivion as a windswept, empty, snowy mountain-top&quot; is bad, and likewise I don't see bleak as synonymous to bad. I see the dramatic potential of something like &quot;emotional desert versus self-destructive turbulence&quot;, but I get that fix from Vampire and Wraith aplenty.<br /> <br /> 4. All in all, if it is executed in a dark romanticist way, I dont see why it is needed when those stories can be told (perhaps better) in Vampire, Wraith and perhaps Werewolf. When it is executed in the &quot;oh no, I'm growing up&quot; way, I find it frankly to miss the mark and be almost insulting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-18T08:13:02-08:00","id":768513,"title":"","content":"Yeah, as a 30-something, even one with a &quot;when will I actually feel like an adult?&quot; sense to my own identity (despite having a good and steady job and just having bought a house), I look at the &quot;loss of childhood&quot; angle as a bit limiting.<br /> <br /> I could actually see that as a great angle to play a cross of C:tD Childlings with nWoD's Innocents, or even as a way to expressly focus C:tD for Childling perspectives.<br /> <br /> But for Wilders, the perspective needs to shift: now, the struggle becomes less about &quot;loss of childhood,&quot; though it still includes elements of &quot;loss of innocence.&quot; It gains the &quot;struggle for identity vs. conformity&quot; angle, however. Wilders are teens and young adults, and &quot;banality&quot; to them is a dread combination of being outcast from the social group AND being lost TO said social group. Almost a stage fright vs. fear of being overlooked issue, combined with a &quot;adult responsibilities are banal&quot; fear coupled to a &quot;adult freedoms are enticing...but cost innocence&quot; concern.<br /> <br /> For Wilders, then, Banality would be about comfort zones and finding the &quot;safe&quot; path, while Glamour is about individuality and taking risks. As even your Glamour starts to erode your Innocence, you need to find other ways to stoke your belief in yourself and your sense of wondrous connection to the Dreaming to stave off Banality. At the same time, clinging too hard to your Innocence becomes a rote rejection of the new, and it, too, starts to make your Glamour fade as Banality sets in: your &quot;faith&quot; becomes a rote recitation of things you are too afraid to grow up and face the truth of, rather than a true childhood sense of acceptance of magic.<br /> <br /> This transition is hard, and fraught with danger, because failing to attempt it is as Banal-death-inducing as trying it and collapsing into a cynical blob. Thus, it appears to many Changelings that growing up is hazardous to their Chimerical health.<br /> <br /> Grumps have navigated those waters, or have undergone Chrysalis VERY late. For them, the struggle is less about identity vs. collective; they are already established and know, at least well enough, who they are and how to value friends and groups versus their own well-being. Mostly. (It's obviously a struggle one's whole life, but we get better at it with age.) They've also faced the demon of &quot;adult responsibilities,&quot; and found that they actually ENABLE the adult freedoms they once craved even while dreading the former.<br /> <br /> For the Grump, the struggle is in reconciling the demands of daily responsibility with the personal yen for grandeur. The fae soul demands More than mundanity. Add to this the same &quot;Masquerade&quot; problems that face the other splats: in a world that doesn't believe in magic, you know it's real and are deeply enmeshed in it. But you also are an adult Changeling; you're practically an elder of their kind. You have adult responsibilities even in the Dreaming world, and those can become sources of monotony and complacency. Complacency is the Banal enemy of the Grump. Complacency and Despondency - a loss of hope for something better. The hazard for the Grump on the Glamour side is almost entirely the descent into madness as one separates from the mundane world.<br /> <br /> Grumps, more than Wilders or Childlings, actively live a double life, and must balance between them lest they lose half of themselves: their magic to the Banality of everyday life, or their sanity and connection to the real world in the magic of the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> Perhaps it IS less tragic if Sir Leigh the Nocker Knight finds himself so enmeshed in the banal joys of his business as a mechanic, the stress and joys of his recent wedding and now taking care of his pregnant wife, and the time spent with his mortal nieces and nephews, that he loses sight of his faerie nature. He's not miserable; he just...forgot...that the &quot;LARP&quot; he used to do with his best friend was more than that. But it still is a loss, and there may come a time when the Faerie world needs Sir Leigh again, so they reach out and try to rekindle his Glamour.<br /> <br /> It certainly is horrific, to Sir Leigh's wife and young son, if their husband and father is acting strangely more and more often, and starts taking risks, vanishing for periods of time, and apparently is involved with something that is drawing vandals to his place of business. If his son complains of monsters scratching at his window, and Lee bravely &quot;fights them off,&quot; that's him being a good father. But his wife may be concerned when Lee maintains a haunted look, like he expects those &quot;monsters&quot; are both real...and likely to come back. What's he involved in?<br /> <br /> The revelation that there's nothing to them, that there isn't a gang of thugs or mafia types or anything, is not particularly reassuring to discover if Lee keeps acting paranoid, and even starts taking extended leaves of absence.<br /> <br /> As Sir Leigh gets more involved in the Dreaming, his mundane life suffers, and he separates himself to spare them...until the Nocker Knight barely recognizes those silly mortals that Lee once called &quot;family,&quot; and finds their needs trivial compared to the great battle he is helping his Freehold fight against the Redcap Menace. Even his fellows may be uncomfortable with his zeal, with his descent into the proclivities of his Nocker nature, as he is less willing to consider real-world impacts and cares only about the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> Again, not AS tragic, to the Changelings; he is &quot;mad&quot; but it's a madness familiar to them. And at least he's happy. Until something Chimerical kills him, and the man left behind is a madness-ridden husk.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Childlings, then, play the game almost as presented, but with a bonus that they don't have to separate their worlds so much. The safety of being a child in the real world, and the acceptance of their &quot;pretend&quot; games, means they can treat both worlds as real, most of the time. Their struggle is, as presented, a loss of faith, belief, and innocence versus a need to protect themselves from the harsh realities of the world by understanding and exploiting them. (And the fact that both worlds can be used against the other is a bonus.)<br /> <br /> Wilders play with the overall WoD tendency to love the teenage wasteland motif, where the struggle for identity vies with the struggle for inclusion. They are in a transition, having to separate their worlds more, and in danger of mistaking it for &quot;childhood&quot; vs. &quot;adulthood,&quot; when in reality their whole paradigm is shifting.<br /> <br /> Grumps play it more straight with the WoD theme of two worlds. They have to maintain a double life and keep up the Secret. For them, it really is a struggle of finding balance: banality is a defense against the madness of glamour-indulgence, but it can kill the magic within. Glamour is power, and joy, and life for the connection to the special world, and can make even the &quot;mundane&quot; world a better place, but garnering it courts insanity, and the beauties of the Dreaming risk losing one's human self entirely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-18T08:22:02-08:00","id":768522,"title":"","content":"i got a copy of changeling around here. i been trying to read it but i just dont get it. the whole idea of roleplaying technically two characters with their own separated lives and such. trying to understand if they are aware of each other and whats the role of these beings in the world of darkness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Forum's Official's Joker and Trickster. Pardon my bad english, aint my first language (I Speak Spanish).<br /> ST: DtF, HtR, WtO, MtA<br /> Signature Chars: Crowley (hakalu), Joe The Nuwisha (WtA)<br /> Changelings: be afraid of the Technocracian High Five of Doom","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1754-crowley","name":"Crowley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1754_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-18T08:29:57-08:00","id":768528,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Segev</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=768513#post768513\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, as a 30-something, even one with a &quot;when will I actually feel like an adult?&quot; sense to my own identity (despite having a good and steady job and just having bought a house), I look at the &quot;loss of childhood&quot; angle as a bit limiting.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that might hit the nail on the head.<br /> <br /> Changeling seems to be written with a specific &quot;rite of passage&quot; in mind, where you clear-cut stop being a child of Wonder and Glamour, and become and adult of Banality.<br /> <br /> My life experience, and the life experience of everyone I know, is not like that. There are no rites of passage, there is only a slow, steady change. Perhaps having that creeping change as the antagonist would help, but one of the reasons that this whole binary child/adult thing rings hollow is because this is not what our life experience is, and the disconnect may be to great to take it seriously.<br /> <br /> I realize this sounds like I am bashing the idea of Changeling. I am not. Most of our great literature is about coming of age and the search for the self. I only &quot;bash&quot; the execution of Changeling as I, an admitted know-nothing, see it.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1754-crowley\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1754_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1754\" data-vbnamecard=\"1754\">Crowley</a> Arent you a Wraith player? Perhaps importing the Psyche/Shadow dichotomy might help you.<br /> <br /> <br /> Second edit: Heh. Reading Segev's post and bringing up Wraith put the idea in my head that Changeling may be as self-and-group therapeutic as Wraith is, just more about forming attachments than letting them go. Any comments from those who actually play it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5238,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","user":"Herbert_West","date":". 11-18-2015, 11:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-18T09:01:41-08:00","id":768545,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crowley</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/761321-changeling-is-not-a-light-game?p=768522#post768522\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">i got a copy of changeling around here. i been trying to read it but i just dont get it. the whole idea of roleplaying technically two characters with their own separated lives and such. trying to understand if they are aware of each other and whats the role of these beings in the world of darkness.</div> </div> </div> </div>The big misconception you have is that you think they're two different characters. They're not. They're one person, in one body. Those who can see the Dreaming see the Fae Mein, which is the Troll or Sidhe or Pooka. Those who can't see only the Mortal Seeming, which is the kid or the youth or the mischievous old man. Changelings often appear a little insane to those who can't see the Dreaming, because they are a kid or a youth or a mischievous old man interacting with things that are not there. The youth ducks under a plenty-high archway, because his Troll mein is just an inch shorter than it and his horns would have gotten caught. The kid is arguing with his stuffed animal, because in the Dreaming it's a chimerical talking cat (which the kid also blames for the broken milk jugs, even though his mom knows there's no way Mr. Whitepaws could have done it, no matter that little Billy left Mr. Whitepaws next to it, nose soaking in the souring milk). The mischievous old man insists he doesn't feed any stray animals, but people see that mangy squirrel and its posse that come and go from his place frequently.<br /> <br /> When interacting with each other, Changelings mostly act like they're the fae their Meins portray, because they see and interact in the Dreaming as much as the real world, and the overlap is almost always more convenient to work with the Dreaming on. They have to pretend to be &quot;normal&quot; around mundane humans, because the Mists conceal the Dreaming from those poor people, and even if they show something supernatural, those mortals will forget in time, and chalk it up to the Changeling being weird and play-acting.<br /> <br /> But Changelings aren't two beings; they're one person, in one body, with two different appearances and acting on two, overlapping worlds with different but distinct dangers. And they have to keep the two straight. Mercifully, at least, the Dreaming's entities can generally SEE the regular world, even if Chimerae can't usually interact with it. So Changelings only look crazy to mortal eyes, not to other fae and Chimerae.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-18T10:23:16-08:00","id":768582,"title":"","content":"I'm 30 next month. I don't understand why the themes of CTD are only for kids? I never particularly played the 'growing up' part of the game. Okay, it's there, but it never seemed as overwhelming as many people here seem to think it is.<br /> <br /> But maybe that's just how we always played it. Which begs the question: who here has actually played the game and how did it work out for you? Our game is very much an urban fantasy one with lots of magic (even though the changelings themselves rarely use cantrips). We focus a lot on the hidden magical places, the secret magical quests, and the forgotten magical wonders that remain like treasure in the corners of the World of Darkness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 11-18-2015, 01:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-18T11:03:35-08:00","id":768610,"title":"","content":"I didn't say it was &quot;only for kids.&quot; I said that the game focuses a lot on themes that seem to want it told from a Childling or a nostalgic Wilder perspective, viewing growing up as a bad thing to be fought. When it needn't; the game can easily be taken in other directions. That's just how the core book seems to focus it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}